<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1848 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Deșteaptă-te, române!</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of Romania: a poem of the 1848 revolution by Andrei Mureșanu, sung to a melody by Anton Pann.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Andrei Mureșanu</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Anton Pann</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Romanians; the revolutionaries of 1848; the Romanian state</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Romanian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Brașov, Romania</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1848; first sung in late June 1848 in Brașov; national anthem of Romania since 24 January 1990</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Andrei Mureșanu, published 1848; melody from Anton Pann's song &quot;Din sânul maicii mele&quot;)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desteapta-te-romane">Deșteaptă-te, române!</a>&quot; (&quot;Awaken Thee, Romanian!&quot;) began as a poem written during the revolution of 1848. Andrei Mureșanu published it that year under the title &quot;Un răsunet&quot; (&quot;An Echo&quot;), as a reply to Vasile Alecsandri's poem &quot;Către Români&quot; (&quot;To <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>&quot;), later known as &quot;Deșteptarea României&quot; (&quot;The Awakening of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>&quot;), from which he took many of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> and motifs; the two poems are much alike. The original text was written in the Romanian Cyrillic alphabet. Mureșanu asked Gheorghe Ucenescu, a church singer of the Șcheii Brașovului quarter of Brașov, to find a melody for it; after Ucenescu sang him several secular tunes, Mureșanu chose instead a sentimental song by Anton Pann, &quot;Din sânul maicii mele&quot;. It was first sung in late June 1848 in the streets of Șcheii Brașovului and at once became the anthem of the revolution. Nicolae Bălcescu called it the &quot;Romanian Marseillaise&quot;.</p><p>It remained a favourite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> and was sung during the country's major conflicts: at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>'s declaration of independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877–1878, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and on the radio, very often, in the days after the coup of 23 August 1944, when Romania turned against Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. It was also used on solemn occasions in the short-lived Moldavian Democratic Republic of 1917–1918.</p><p>After the Communist Party abolished the monarchy on 30 December 1947, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desteapta-te-romane">Deșteaptă-te, române!</a>&quot; and other patriotic songs tied to the old regime were outlawed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolae-ceausescu">Nicolae Ceaușescu</a>'s government later allowed it to be played and sung in public, but it was never the anthem of the Socialist Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. It was sung during the anti-communist revolution of December 1989, and on 24 January 1990 it became the national anthem, replacing the communist-era &quot;Trei culori&quot; (&quot;Three Colours&quot;). From 1991 to 1994 it was also the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a>, which then replaced it with &quot;Limba noastră&quot; (&quot;Our Language&quot;).</p><p>The poem has eleven stanzas. By Romanian law the first, second, fourth and last are sung on official occasions; the full version is sung at major events such as the National Holiday on 1 December, with a 21-gun salute when the President is present, while short versions, as at sporting events, keep only the first stanza and the first half of the second. 29 July is observed each year as National Anthem Day.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A call to action: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> are urged to wake from a long sleep under oppression and seize their freedom now or never, taking courage from the memory of their ancestors and standing together as one nation. The mood is urgent and stirring.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Romanian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Romanian</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Deșteaptă-te, române, din somnul cel de moarte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Awake, Romanian, from the sleep of death,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">În care te-adânciră barbarii de tirani</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>into which barbarous tyrants have sunk you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Acum ori niciodată croiește-ți altă soarte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>now or never fashion yourself another fate,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">La care să se-nchine și cruzii tăi dușmani.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to which even your cruel enemies shall bow.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Acum ori niciodată să dăm dovezi în lume</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now or never let us give proofs to the world</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Că-n aste mâni mai curge un sânge de roman,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that in these hands there still flows a Roman blood,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Și că-n a noastre piepturi păstrăm cu fală-un nume</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and that in our breasts we keep with pride a name</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Triumfător în lupte, un nume de Traian!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>triumphant in battles, the name of Trajan!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Înalță-ți lata frunte și cată-n giur de tine,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Raise your broad brow and look around you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Cum stau ca brazi în munte voinicii sute de mii;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>how the brave lads stand like firs on the mountain, hundreds of thousands;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Un glas ei mai așteaptă și sar ca lupi în stâne,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>they wait only for a call and they leap like wolves into the sheepfolds,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Bătrâni, bărbați, juni, tineri, din munți și din câmpii!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>old men, men, youths, young people, from the mountains and from the plains!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Priviți, mărețe umbre, Mihai, Ștefan, Corvine,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Behold, great shades, Mihai, Ștefan, Corvinus,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Româna națiune, ai voștri strănepoți,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Romanian nation, your great-grandchildren,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Cu brațele armate, cu focul vostru-n vine,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with arms bearing weapons, with your fire in their veins,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">„Viață-n libertate ori moarte” strigă toți.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“Life in liberty or death” all cry.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 44 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Pre voi vă nimiciră a pizmei răutate</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You were destroyed by the malice of envy</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Și oarba neunire la Milcov și Carpați!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the blind disunion at the Milcov and the Carpathians!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Dar noi, pătrunși la suflet de sfânta libertate,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But we, pierced to the soul by holy liberty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Jurăm că vom da mâna, să fim pururea frați!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>swear that we will join hands, to be brothers for ever!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">O mamă văduvită de la Mihai cel Mare</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A mother widowed since Mihai the Great</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Pretinde de la fii-și azi mână d-ajutori,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>asks of her sons today a helping hand,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Și blastămă cu lacrămi în ochi pe orișicare,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and curses with tears in her eyes anyone at all</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">În astfel de pericul s-ar face vânzători!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who in such a danger would turn traitor!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">De fulgere să piară, de trăsnet și pucioasă,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>By lightning let him perish, by thunderbolt and brimstone,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Oricare s-ar retrage din gloriosul loc,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>whoever would draw back from the glorious place,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Când patria sau mama, cu inima duioasă,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>when the homeland or the mother, with tender heart,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Va cere ca să trecem prin sabie și foc!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>shall ask that we pass through sword and fire!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">N-ajunge iataganul barbarei semilune,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Is it not enough, the yataghan of the barbarous crescent,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">A cărui plăgi fatale și azi le mai simțim;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>whose fatal wounds we still feel even today;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Acum se vâră cnuta în vetrele străbune,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>now the knout is thrust into our ancestral hearths,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Dar martor ne e Domnul că vii nu oprimim!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>but the Lord is our witness that alive we will not accept it [?]!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">N-ajunge despotismul cu-ntreaga lui orbie,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Is it not enough, despotism with all its blindness,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Al cărui jug de seculi ca vitele-l purtăm;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>whose yoke we have borne for centuries like cattle;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Acum se-ncearcă cruzii, cu oarba lor trufie,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>now the cruel ones try, in their blind arrogance,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Să ne răpească limba, dar morți numai o dăm!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to rob us of our language, but only dead will we give it up!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Români din patru unghiuri, acum ori niciodată</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Romanians from the four corners, now or never</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Uniți-vă în cuget, uniți-vă-n simțiri!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>unite in thought, unite in feeling!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Strigați în lumea largă că Dunărea-i furată</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Cry out to the wide world that the Danube is stolen</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Prin intrigă și silă, viclene uneltiri!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>through intrigue and force, cunning schemings!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Preoți, cu crucea-n frunte căci oastea e creștină,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Priests, with the cross at the head, for the army is Christian,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Deviza-i libertate și scopul ei preasfânt.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>its motto is liberty and its aim most holy.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Murim mai bine-n luptă, cu glorie deplină,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Better we die in battle, in full glory,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ro">Decât să fim sclavi iarăși în vechiul nost’pământ!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>than be slaves again in our old land!</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Andrei Mureșanu (1848). Text from <a href="https://ro.wikisource.org/wiki/De%C8%99teapt%C4%83-te%2C_rom%C3%A2ne!" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (15)</summary><ul><li>line 1.2: 'barbarii de tirani' is literally 'the barbarians of tyrants', i.e. barbarous tyrants.</li><li>line 1.3: 'croiește-ți' is 'cut out, fashion for yourself' (as a garment is cut).</li><li>line 3.1: 'cată-n giur' is the older spelling of 'caută-n jur', 'look around'.</li><li>line 3.4: 'juni' and 'tineri' both mean 'young (people)': 'juni' is the older, bookish word.</li><li>line 4.1: Mihai is Michael the Brave, Ștefan is Stephen the Great, Corvine (vocative) is Corvinus, the name of the Hunyadi (John Hunyadi or his son Matthias Corvinus; the line does not say which).</li><li>line 4.2: 'strănepoți' is 'great-grandchildren', here 'descendants'.</li><li>line 5.1: 'Pre voi' is the older form of 'pe voi', 'you' (object).</li><li>line 5.2: the Milcov is a river that formed the border between Wallachia and Moldavia; the Carpathians divided them from Transylvania.</li><li>line 6.2: 'mână d-ajutori' is 'a helping hand' (literally 'a hand of help').</li><li>line 6.3: 'blastămă' is the older form of 'blestemă', 'curses'.</li><li>line 8.1: 'iataganul barbarei semilune' is 'the yataghan of the barbarous crescent': the Ottoman sword.</li><li>line 8.3: 'cnuta' is the knout, the Russian whip.</li><li>line 8.4: 'vii nu oprimim' as printed; read as 'vii n-o primim' ('alive we will not accept it'). Taken as one word, 'oprimim' would be 'we oppress', which gives no sense here.</li><li>line 10.1: 'din patru unghiuri' is 'from the four corners'.</li><li>line 11.4: 'nost'' is the elided 'nostru', 'our'.</li></ul></details></section>
